<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Šabac</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Szabács</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Šabac is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> river. Its fortress was built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1471, taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> in 1476 and held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> as part of its southern defences until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> captured it, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, in 1521. In 1806 the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-misar">Battle of Mišar</a> was an early Serbian victory over the Ottomans in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-serbian-uprising">First Serbian Uprising</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sabac.jpg" alt="Šabac" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ванилица · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zgrada_Trijumf,_%C5%A0abac_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Šabac is the administrative centre of the Mačva District in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and the traditional centre of the fertile Mačva region, on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a>. The name is first mentioned in Ragusan documents of 1454, and its origin is uncertain; it may come from the name of the river. The city has been known as Zaslon in medieval Serbian, Szabács in Hungarian, Böğürdelen in Turkish and Schabatz in German.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>There is evidence of permanent settlement from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> a Slavic settlement named Zaslon stood on the site, part of the Serbian Despotate until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1459. The Ottomans built a fortress called Beyerdelen, meaning side-striker. The Hungarian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> captured it in 1476, and it stayed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> until the Ottomans retook it in 1521. Under Ottoman rule it was a typical oriental town with tiny streets, small shops and several mosques, with Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and smaller numbers of Hungarians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>.</p><p>Control passed several times between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy. Habsburg rule began in 1718 and ended after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-belgrade">Treaty of Belgrade</a> in 1739; a second period began in 1789, when the storming of the city was one of the early experiences of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-poniatowski">Józef Poniatowski</a>. In 1806, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-serbian-uprising">First Serbian Uprising</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karadorde">Karađorđe</a> led the insurgents to one of their first victories over the Ottoman army near the village of Mišar. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-serbian-uprising">Second Serbian Uprising</a> in 1815 Šabac joined the autonomous Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obrenovic-dynasty">Obrenović dynasty</a>, and from 1820 to 1850 Jevrem Obrenović, the brother of Prince Miloš, helped found a hospital, a pharmacy, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and a musical society. The Ottoman army evacuated the fort in 1867.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the Austro-Hungarian army occupied and devastated Šabac, halving its population from about 14,000 to 7,000 on the orders of Kasimir von Lütgendorf. During the German occupation of 1941 to 1944 its 1,200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were arrested and sent on the Kladovo transport, and reprisals after an attack on the German garrison left 1,130 civilians executed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-partisans">Yugoslav Partisans</a> liberated the city in 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Šabac grew after the war into an industrial city around the Zorka chemical plant, which had opened in 1938. A new bridge was built over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> and the swamp of Benska Bara was drained and turned into a residential neighbourhood. The city's administrative area had 105,432 inhabitants at the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and agriculture, food production and raspberry growing are important in the area.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Šabac” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
